.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt UMASK 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm umask
.Nd set the file creation mask
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/stat.h
.Ft mode_t
.Fn umask "mode_t mask"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn umask
sets the calling thread's file creation mask to
.Fa mask
and returns the previous mask.
Threads made by
.Xr clone 2
with
.Dv CLONE_FS ,
as glibc's and musl's
.Fn pthread_create
make them, share the mask, as they share the working directory; a thread
made without it, and a child of
.Xr fork 2 ,
gets a copy.
Only the low nine permission bits are kept:
.Ql "mask & 0777" .
.Pp
On KRFFS, including the boot root, and on ext2, new files and directories
receive the requested permission bits with the process mask removed.
For example, a mask of 0027 changes requested permissions 0666 to 0640
and 0777 to 0750.
FAT32 and exFAT ignore the creation mode and report permissions from
their mount mask and read-only attribute; the process mask does not
determine their permissions.
.Pp
The i386 system call number is 60.
EBX contains the new mask.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn umask
returns the previous mask.
On failure, musl returns
.Ql (mode_t)-1
and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/miscprobe.c
sets the mask to 0027, checks that the previous mask was 0022, and
restores 0022, checking that the returned mask is 0027.
.Pa tests/ownprobe.c
checks that a mask of 0027 produces a file with permissions 0640 from
0666 and a directory with permissions 0750 from 0777.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ESRCH]"
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
